-- **[Session Liveness] Heartbeat file primary; pid + process start time decide `gone`, and `claude agents --json` only enriches it.** Rationale: the prototype's heartbeat proved reliable. A bare pid is unsafe because pids get reused, which is why start time is matched too: a snapshot whose pid is dead, or alive with another start time, is `gone` whatever the heartbeat says (Session liveness). `claude agents --json` adds idle/busy status and shows unwrapped sessions as read-only rows. A `viola run` whose `ViolaName` is `live` or `stale` refuses to start (exit 1, with a message naming the instance); once the endpoint exists, a name with an answering endpoint refuses the same way. `viola revive` refuses a `live` or `stale` name through the same collision check and its two stderr pairs, as the first of its three preflight readings (the name's holder, the logged session, the recorded directory; a later reading is not taken once an earlier one refuses). The strict-modes check on the home and the instance's files runs ahead of them, before the wrapper log opens (chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor). A revive that passes goes through the same start as `run`, so the collision check runs twice. It resumes the `gone` instance in place: its `events.ndjson` is appended to with the earlier bytes unchanged, no launch is replayed, and its first record is `wheel{holder:"driver", cause:"start"}`, as every start's (the founder's answer, live, 2026-10-10, relayed by the operator: a revived start keeps the wheel at `driver`). Otherwise `run` starts in this order: program resolution (the `.cmd`/`.bat` refusal, which writes nothing), collision check, pinned copy and plugin folder, the ConPTY sideload (Windows x64, fail-open), version gate, exclusive bind of the endpoint (a bind that finds the name taken is an exit-1 refusal, `squatted-name`, so two concurrent starts cannot both win), first snapshot (with `endpoint`, `pinned_bin`, and `statusline_command` when the named source gave a command; `pid`, `started_at`) and heartbeat, the start `wheel` and `budget-gate` events, then the child spawn, after which the snapshot is rewritten with `child_pid`. Between the first snapshot and the spawn `run` also rewrites the per-session settings override (Unix only; §Occupied Resources, Claude Code integration names). As landed by 2026-09-28-capability-ledger-and-viola-verify every step has its place (the sideload step added by 2026-09-29-sideloaded-conpty): program resolution → collision → pinned copy and plugin → ConPTY sideload (`run.conpty_sideload`, Windows: `pin_companions` + the absolute-path pre-load; `outcome` `loaded` · `hash-mismatch` · `unreadable` · `load-failed` · `not-built`, never a refusal; its held handles live until `spawn_child` returns) → strip plan → version gate (`run::version_gate`: the bounded `--version` read logged as a `version-probe` `process-start`/`process-exit` pair, the stamps read, `cli_version`/`cli_verified` carried into the first snapshot and the `claude-child` `process-start`; `run` prints nothing on any gate outcome) → endpoint bind → the budget gate at start (span `run.budget_eval` with `trigger: start`; it reads `budget.json`, restores no override and writes nothing; the thresholds were read from `config.json` earlier in the start, each rejection logged then) → first snapshot (its `budget_paused` is that verdict, its `budget_override_until` always absent) → heartbeat → start events (record two, `budget-gate`, carries that verdict's `data`, where it carried the constant `{"paused": false}` before chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor) → spawn, so the child's first SessionStart hook finds a listening endpoint and a recorded snapshot. Since chunk 2026-10-10-session-links the start also restores the wrapper's link set before the endpoint serves: it reads `events.ndjson` twice there (`WaitFeed::rebuild`, then `LinkSet::restore`), the first snapshot's `links` is the restored set, the start appends no line for links and its start records are unchanged. `viola revive` starts through the same function, so a revived start's first snapshot carries the logged links. A gone instance's directory is reused at once, and its `events.ndjson` is appended to, never truncated.
+- **[Session Liveness] Heartbeat file primary; pid + process start time decide `gone`, and `claude agents --json` only enriches it.** Rationale: the prototype's heartbeat proved reliable. A bare pid is unsafe because pids get reused, which is why start time is matched too: a snapshot whose pid is dead, or alive with another start time, is `gone` whatever the heartbeat says (Session liveness). `claude agents --json` adds idle/busy status and shows unwrapped sessions as read-only rows. A `viola run` whose `ViolaName` is `live` or `stale` refuses to start (exit 1, with a message naming the instance); once the endpoint exists, a name with an answering endpoint refuses the same way. `viola revive` refuses a `live` or `stale` name through the same collision check and its two stderr pairs, as the first of its four preflight readings (the name's holder, the logged session, `session-live`, the recorded directory; a later reading is not taken once an earlier one refuses). `session-live` (chunk 2026-10-16-unwrapped-sessions-on-the-board) is one `claude agents --json` read: a row whose `sessionId` is the logged id about to be resumed refuses, `--fork` skips the reading, and a failed read passes (fail open). As measured on 2.1.287 on the Linux dev host (that chunk's `evidence/live-p4.md`), a second interactive `claude --resume <id>` of an id a live `claude` holds is neither refused nor copied: both sessions run under the one id, two writers on one transcript, so the reading stays a refusal; and after `/clear` the agents row of the session's pid names the new id (P12 follows), so a session-id join survives a `/clear` because the wrapper rewrites the snapshot's `agent_session_id` on each `session-start`. The strict-modes check on the home and the instance's files runs ahead of them, before the wrapper log opens (chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor). A revive that passes goes through the same start as `run`, so the collision check runs twice. It resumes the `gone` instance in place: its `events.ndjson` is appended to with the earlier bytes unchanged, no launch is replayed, and its first record is `wheel{holder:"driver", cause:"start"}`, as every start's (the founder's answer, live, 2026-10-10, relayed by the operator: a revived start keeps the wheel at `driver`). Otherwise `run` starts in this order: program resolution (the `.cmd`/`.bat` refusal, which writes nothing), collision check, pinned copy and plugin folder, the ConPTY sideload (Windows x64, fail-open), version gate, exclusive bind of the endpoint (a bind that finds the name taken is an exit-1 refusal, `squatted-name`, so two concurrent starts cannot both win), first snapshot (with `endpoint`, `pinned_bin`, and `statusline_command` when the named source gave a command; `pid`, `started_at`) and heartbeat, the start `wheel` and `budget-gate` events, then the child spawn, after which the snapshot is rewritten with `child_pid`. Between the first snapshot and the spawn `run` also rewrites the per-session settings override (Unix only; §Occupied Resources, Claude Code integration names). As landed by 2026-09-28-capability-ledger-and-viola-verify every step has its place (the sideload step added by 2026-09-29-sideloaded-conpty): program resolution → collision → pinned copy and plugin → ConPTY sideload (`run.conpty_sideload`, Windows: `pin_companions` + the absolute-path pre-load; `outcome` `loaded` · `hash-mismatch` · `unreadable` · `load-failed` · `not-built`, never a refusal; its held handles live until `spawn_child` returns) → strip plan → version gate (`run::version_gate`: the bounded `--version` read logged as a `version-probe` `process-start`/`process-exit` pair, the stamps read, `cli_version`/`cli_verified` carried into the first snapshot and the `claude-child` `process-start`; `run` prints nothing on any gate outcome) → endpoint bind → the budget gate at start (span `run.budget_eval` with `trigger: start`; it reads `budget.json`, restores no override and writes nothing; the thresholds were read from `config.json` earlier in the start, each rejection logged then) → first snapshot (its `budget_paused` is that verdict, its `budget_override_until` always absent) → heartbeat → start events (record two, `budget-gate`, carries that verdict's `data`, where it carried the constant `{"paused": false}` before chunk 2026-10-10-budget-governor) → spawn, so the child's first SessionStart hook finds a listening endpoint and a recorded snapshot. Since chunk 2026-10-10-session-links the start also restores the wrapper's link set before the endpoint serves: it reads `events.ndjson` twice there (`WaitFeed::rebuild`, then `LinkSet::restore`), the first snapshot's `links` is the restored set, the start appends no line for links and its start records are unchanged. `viola revive` starts through the same function, so a revived start's first snapshot carries the logged links. A gone instance's directory is reused at once, and its `events.ndjson` is appended to, never truncated.
 - **[GUI Control Scope]** The frontend is deferred to 0.2.0; its technology is not chosen. As the 0.2.0 frontend's starting intent, the page shows each session row (wrapped, or read-only unwrapped), its liveness (`live` · `stale`), idle/busy status, wheel holder and any pending dialog. It also shows the budget reading and its age, the link set, the `skipped` counts, and a live event feed.
